edb/ref 17592186055609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054931 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054931 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054931 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054931 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186055613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054931 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054931 #edb/ref 1045 "Factoring Trinomials" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054931 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186055608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054931 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054931 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "581ada9e-b8f9-5513-9a35-8ae3af36139e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055608 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055608 #edb/ref 1061 178072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055608 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "add682be-a6dd-51b0-a261-ba8a3f84b958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055608 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055609 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055609 #edb/ref 1061 178073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055609 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "072ef290-5a3e-575d-a0fd-7d8084343f23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055609 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055610 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055610 #edb/ref 1061 178077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "232a2780-b6bf-56f2-b607-6b8d27ae3f25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055611 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055611 #edb/ref 1061 178078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055611 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "47e26748-840d-518f-8679-595bc9b0406d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055611 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055612 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055612 #edb/ref 1061 178082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055612 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "47589875-8a7f-5cea-a2cf-0da701bb35ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055612 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186056076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055613 #edb/ref 1061 178083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87945626-63e1-5990-b1f8-6d13138cf85c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056069 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's think about how to factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 7x + 12\n$$\n\nThis trinomial is neither a perfect square nor a difference of squares. In such cases, the factoring process requires a little trial and error.\n\nWe can factor this trinomial in four steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$:The factor pairs of $12$ are\n$(1, 12), (2, 6), (3, 4)$.\n- **Step 2:** Determine which of these factor pairs add up to the coefficient of the linear term $(7)$:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 12|= 13 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + 6|= 8 & \\cdot \\\\ 3 + 4|= 7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 3:** Using the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $7x=3x+4x$:\n$x^{2} + 7x + 12|=; x^{2} + 3x + 4x_(⏟)_(7x) + 12$\n- **Step 4:** Factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n$x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12|=; (x^{2} + 3x) + (4x + 12)|=; x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3)|=; (x + 4)(x + 3)$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nBefore we continue, note the following:\n\n- The process carried out in step 4 is sometimes called **factoring by grouping.** This is because we group two sets of terms and factor them individually before factoring the entire expression.\n- We can check that our answer is correct by multiplying out the factors:\n$(x + 4)(x + 3)|= x(x + 3) + 4(x + 3); = x^{2} + 3x + 4x + 12; = x^{2} + 7x + 12✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056069 #edb/ref 1065 8891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056069 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "90a8aa34-4549-57c2-bdc2-24cc962cd306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056069 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056070 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now consider the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 8\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant term $(-8)$ is *negative*. So, when writing down our factor pairs, one number will always be positive, and the other will always be negative.\n\nSo, first, we write down all factor pairs of $-8{:}$\n\n$$\n(1,-8), (-1, 8), (2,-4), (-2, 4)\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + (-8) &= -7 & \\cdot \\\\ (-1) + 8|= 7 & \\cdot \\\\ 2 + (-4)|=-2 & ✓ \\\\ (-2) + 4|= 2 & \\cdot\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-2x = 2x-4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 2x - 8 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2x - 4x - 8 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} + 2x) + (-4x - 8) &=  \\\\\nx(x + 2) - 4(x + 2) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056070 #edb/ref 1065 8894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056070 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3b5a5752-232b-5714-b74a-1e50c4210b83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056070 #edb/ref 1067 "Trinomials With Negative Constants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056076 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that to factor a quadratic expression, we must find a factor pair of the constant term that adds to the coefficient of the linear term.\n\nWith that in mind, let's factor the following trinomial:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 7x+12\n$$\n\nNotice that,\n\n- the constant term $(12)$ is *positive*, while\n- the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$ is *negative*.\n\nSince the constant is *positive* and the coefficient of the linear term is *negative*, we must first consider the *negative* factor pairs of the constant term $(12)$. These are as follows:\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\qquad (-2,-6),\\qquad (-3,-4)\n$$\n\nFor each pair, their *product* is positive, and their *sum* is negative, which is what we want.\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}(-1) + (-12) &= -13 & \\cdot \\\\ (-2) + (-6)|=-8 & \\cdot \\\\ (-3) + (-4)|=-7 & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as\n\n$$\n-7x = -3x - 4x\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 7x + 12 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 3x - 4x + 12 &=  \\\\\n(x^{2} - 3x) + (-4x + 12) &=  \\\\\n&= x(x - 3) + 4(-x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to get a *common* factor in both terms, we take an additional factor of $-1$ from the second term:\n\n$$\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3)\n$$\n\nNow that we have a common factor of $(x-3)$, we factor the expression fully in the usual way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 3) - 4(x - 3) &=  \\\\\n&= (x - 3)(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056076 #edb/ref 1065 9504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056076 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "25f481d5-38d8-5f35-95fc-dd74f97d0394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186056076 #edb/ref 1067 "Trinomials With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064322 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064322 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064322 #edb/ref 1039 "Factoring a Monomial out of a Binomial Containing Two Variables" #edb/ref 1759218